<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  UTF-8 process code page application manifest, embedded in the three apps.

  The element and its namespace are the ones Microsoft documents for `activeCodePage`
  (win32/sbscs/application-manifests): `UTF-8` is the only value valid on Windows 10 and is
  case-sensitive, and the element was first added in Windows 10 version 1903 (May 2019 Update).
  Declaring it and running on an earlier build is allowed, and there the process keeps its legacy
  code page, so the element is an opt-in the system honours where it exists rather than a guarantee
  everywhere.

  It is a default rather than a measured option (unlike `cmake/segment_heap.manifest`) because it
  repairs an observable defect and has no performance arm to compare against. These apps write UTF-8
  through `printf` and `std::cout`, and no code of theirs sets a console code page: `WriteConsoleW`,
  `wprintf`, `wmain` and `_setmode` all have zero occurrences under apps/, so before this manifest the
  CLI's generated text depended on the console the user happened to open. With it the process code
  page is UTF-8, so narrow output, `main`'s arguments and the narrow file APIs agree with the bytes
  the rest of this tree already writes. diffutils ships the same file under the same name
  (`windows-utf8.manifest`), which is the same pattern reached independently.

  Namespace strings are silent-fail: an unrecognised `windowsSettings` namespace is ignored rather
  than reported, so the URL below is the documented one for this element and is deliberately not the
  2020 namespace used by `cmake/segment_heap.manifest` for `heapType`. The file itself is not
  silent-fail: the linker parses it, so a malformed XML comment (a `--` sequence in prose is invalid)
  fails the build with mt.exe c1010070 rather than being dropped.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0"
          xmlns:asmv3="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
  <asmv3:application>
    <asmv3:windowsSettings xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">
      <activeCodePage>UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </asmv3:windowsSettings>
  </asmv3:application>
</assembly>
